
Simply put:

    Validate your form against your actual backend rules in real time, without submitting the form.

Before Precognition you had two choices:

    Duplicate validation rules in frontend (pain to maintain)
    Submit form and wait for 422 error (bad UX)

Precognition solves both. Your frontend talks to your backend validation in real time as the user types.
How It Works Under The Hood

User types in field
→ Precognition sends lightweight request to your endpoint
→ Laravel runs ONLY validation (skips actual handler logic)
→ Returns validation result instantly
→ Frontend shows error or success immediately
→ No form submission happened yet

The key is that special header:

Precognition: true

When Laravel sees this header it runs validation and stops. Never touches your handle() logic.
Setup
Install Frontend Package

For Vue:
bash

npm install laravel-precognition-vue

For React:
bash

npm install laravel-precognition-react

For vanilla JS:
bash

npm install laravel-precognition

Backend Setup

Just add the middleware to your route:
php

// routes/api.php
Route::post('/register', [RegisteredUserController::class, 'store'])
    ->middleware(['web', HandlePrecognitiveRequests::class]);

Or in your controller:
php

use Illuminate\Foundation\Http\Middleware\HandlePrecognitiveRequests;

class RegisteredUserController extends Controller
{
    public function __construct()
    {
        $this->middleware(HandlePrecognitiveRequests::class);
    }
}

That's literally all the backend needs. Your existing validation just works.
Real Example With Your Register Controller

Your controller stays exactly as is:
php

public function store(Request $request): Response
{
    $request->validate([
        'name'     => ['required', 'string', 'max:255'],
        'email'    => ['required', 'string', 'lowercase', 'email', 'max:255', 'unique:'.User::class],
        'password' => ['required', 'confirmed', Rules\Password::defaults()],
    ]);

    $user = User::create([
        'name'     => $request->name,
        'email'    => $request->email,
        'password' => Hash::make($request->string('password')),
    ]);

    event(new Registered($user));
    Auth::login($user);

    return response()->noContent();
}

Nothing changes. Precognition just intercepts before User::create runs.
Frontend Implementation
Vue Example
vue

<script setup>
import { useForm } from 'laravel-precognition-vue'

const form = useForm('post', '/api/v1/register', {
    name: '',
    email: '',
    password: '',
    password_confirmation: '',
})

const submit = () => form.submit()
</script>

<template>
  <form @submit.prevent="submit">

    <!-- Name Field -->
    <div>
      <input
        v-model="form.name"
        type="text"
        placeholder="Name"
        @change="form.validate('name')"
      />
      <span v-if="form.invalid('name')">
        {{ form.errors.name }}
      </span>
    </div>

    <!-- Email Field -->
    <div>
      <input
        v-model="form.email"
        type="email"
        placeholder="Email"
        @change="form.validate('email')"
      />
      <span v-if="form.invalid('email')">
        {{ form.errors.email }}
      </span>
    </div>

    <!-- Password Field -->
    <div>
      <input
        v-model="form.password"
        type="password"
        placeholder="Password"
        @change="form.validate('password')"
      />
      <span v-if="form.invalid('password')">
        {{ form.errors.password }}
      </span>
    </div>

    <!-- Password Confirmation -->
    <div>
      <input
        v-model="form.password_confirmation"
        type="password"
        placeholder="Confirm Password"
      />
    </div>

    <button
      type="submit"
      :disabled="form.processing"
    >
      {{ form.processing ? 'Registering...' : 'Register' }}
    </button>

  </form>
</template>

What Each Thing Does

form.validate('email')     → triggers real time validation for that field
form.invalid('email')      → returns true if field has error
form.errors.email          → the actual error message from your backend
form.processing            → true while form is submitting
form.submit()              → actually submits the form for real

The Email Unique Check Magic

This is where Precognition really shines.

When user types their email and leaves the field:

    Precognition fires a request to your backend
    Your backend runs unique:users validation against real database
    Returns "The email has already been taken" instantly
    User sees it before even clicking submit

No duplicate backend logic. No custom AJAX check. Just works.
Useful Form Methods
javascript

// Validate single field
form.validate('email')

// Validate multiple fields
form.validate(['email', 'name'])

// Check if field is valid
form.valid('email')      // true if passed validation
form.invalid('email')    // true if failed validation

// Check if field has been touched
form.touched('email')    // true if user interacted with field

// Reset form
form.reset()

// Reset specific fields
form.reset('email', 'password')

// Check if form is dirty (changed from initial)
form.dirty('email')

With Inertia.js (Even Cleaner)

If you use Inertia the package is slightly different:
bash

npm install laravel-precognition-vue-inertia

vue

<script setup>
import { useForm } from 'laravel-precognition-vue-inertia'

const form = useForm('post', '/register', {
    name: '',
    email: '',
    password: '',
    password_confirmation: '',
})

const submit = () => form.submit({
    onSuccess: () => form.reset(),
})
</script>

Inertia version handles redirects automatically after successful submission. Perfect for your stack.
Debouncing (Important for Performance)

You don't want a request firing on every single keystroke. Add debounce:
vue

<input
  v-model="form.email"
  type="email"
  @input="form.validate.debounce(500, 'email')"
/>

Now it waits 500ms after user stops typing before firing. Much better UX and less server load.
When to Use Precognition

Perfect for:

    Registration forms (unique email check)
    Complex validation rules you don't want to duplicate
    Long forms where early feedback matters
    Any form where backend validation is the source of truth

Overkill for:

    Simple contact forms
    Search inputs
    Single field forms

Real World Flow Summary

User types email "roshan@gmail.com"
→ Leaves field (onChange fires)
→ Precognition sends: POST /api/v1/register
  Header: Precognition: true
  Header: Precognition-Validate-Only: email
  Body: { email: "roshan@gmail.com" }
→ Laravel validates email only
→ Returns 204 (valid) or 422 (invalid)
→ UI shows checkmark or error instantly
→ User fills rest of form
→ Clicks submit
→ form.submit() sends real request
→ Full validation + User::create() runs
→ Done

Clean. No duplication. Great UX.